Z||000014||ginoferrari||07-18-2001||03:12 PM||||Is that your conversion Roger? It looks good, I want to try one some day. I was in the process of buying the GTO when my last wife had divorce papers served on me. I not only didn't get the car, I lost a $350k waterfront house too. I really think the Washington 50/50 divorce laws lean towards the woman a little to much. But it did drive really fine. Since then I've found out more about the maintenance and insurance on the GTO and I'm kind of glad I didn't get it. Thanks to all that helped me get a photo on my signature, I'm using imagestation. I would want to drive the GTO all the time anyway and wouldn't have time for work. And I'd never want to park it anywhere either.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/p04280ca3c615c9547bd6719c3b5a778f/fe5845bc.jpg">                   <BR>84 2M4<BR>84 SE<BR>87 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||03:28 PM||||yes thats mine. Looks different now. going to take a bunch of new pics this weekend. A girl I know is going with me to the lake to do some for me. The whole reason I made the kit instead of buying the real one was that cost of maintainance. I drive mine a lot and at there recommended service id have to spend around $15,000 @ year just for that. The insurance for me is not that bad. No accidents, just a few minor speed tickets (less than 10 over) Ill be at Pittsburgh Gran Prix this saturday for the car show, then meeting Danielle sunday for pics. (I may have to wear a diaper, LOL, shes that hot...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.....)<P>yes formula, the GTO is longer between the wheel well and door. it has a longitudinal flat 12 cyl, twin turbocharged. Has the same basic body lines of the 308/328 though. The dead givaways of the GTO are the clear quarter glass, longer body and the pedestal mirrors. The louvers in quarter behind the rear wheel are only on GTOs too, I may do those next winter on mine. They work like louvers/heat extractors on the older Firebird TAs front fenders.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Jay||07-18-2001||04:15 PM||||Roger you are correct, except that the GTO is an 8 cylinder not a 12.  It is mounted longitudinal.  It's the engine that was used in the GTO "evoluzione" and a more refined version powers the F40.  If you were considering a real GTO what was the price they were asking?  There are only about 272 real GTO's out there.  Many have converted the 308's to look like GTO's and have added twin turbos but the engine remains transverse.<P>You don't see this out the back of a 308!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gto.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||07-18-2001||07:28 PM||||your rite, my booboo. I was thinking of the Boxer engine. GTO is a V8 if I remember about 175 cu in. with 2 IHI turbos w/intercoolers, around 400 hp. I looked at both 308/328 and the GTO. If I remember I think one I found for sale was  around $100,000. Lots of low miles 308/328s in the $25k-$35ks. I got near that in my kit.......but I can drive it and not worry about being stranded in bumf**k for days waiting for a part. Ive seen some 308 conversions, and some replicas of the GTO with the louvers too. About your rear view-yea you dont see that transaxle. The 308s have the louvered panel between the exhausts.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
